  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records point toward several ingredients of the later result but do not disclose their decisive combination. R6 provides the strongest anticipation: a highly accurate machine-learning interatomic potential for the closely related BCC refractory MoNbTaW alloy and an explicit treatment of SRO. R8 and R16 connect atomistic potentials or first-principles calculations with ordering in multicomponent alloys, while R2, R4, and R10 establish that finite-temperature phase stability depends on Gibbs-energy contributions. R15 further demonstrates consequential SRO in another refractory HEA, and R19 applies atomistic simulation to thermal evolution and melting of a multicomponent alloy. Nevertheless, none of the records proposes replacing thermodynamic integration with MTP-to-DFT free-energy perturbation, exploits the low variance of MTP–DFT energy differences, or connects a Fermi-level DOS gap and SRO stabilization quantitatively to melting-temperature shifts. An expert could plausibly seek an ML-potential-based acceleration of refractory-alloy thermodynamics, but the specific FEP workflow and coupled electronic/SRO melting mechanism would require a substantial new idea."
